## SECTION 3 — AOA STAGES TABLE
<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing a large picture of different weather conditions (sunny, rainy, cloudy, windy) and a simple weekly weather chart. Students look at the pictures and identify the weather. Teacher asks, "What's the weather like today?" "Is it sunny or cloudy?" (Review vocabulary from Lesson 1: <i>sunny, cloudy, rainy, windy, cold, warm</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher points to the chart and says, "Look at this weather <b>forecast</b> for tomorrow. It <b>will be sunny</b>." (Modeling future simple tense and intonation on the last word). The teacher points to a cloud on the chart and says, "This is a <b>cloud</b>." Then, the teacher models segmenting a multisyllabic word by clapping the syllables: "tem-pe-ra-ture" or "fore-cast."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If it's 'sunny,' can you see the sun?" (Expected answer: "Yes!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does 'will be' mean now or in the future?" (Expected answer: "In the future!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Weather Report".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated weather report text for a city, including a simple bar graph or icon-based chart showing temperature (<i>warm, cold</i>) and weather conditions (<i>rain, sun, wind</i>) for 3-4 days (Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday). The text will integrate grammar (future simple, prepositions of place) and vocabulary (<i>weather, forecast, report, cloud, storm, rain, wind, sun, temperature, prediction</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Reading: Interpret simple charts and graphs related to texts. Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by segmenting multisyllabic words into syllables.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher displays the weather report. First, students listen as the teacher reads the text aloud, pointing to the corresponding parts in the chart.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is this text about?" (Expected answer: "The weather forecast.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> Students read the text silently. Teacher provides 3-4 True/False statements about the main idea (e.g., "The weather report is for next week." "Tomorrow will be hot."). Students mark T or F.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match weather icons (sun, cloud, rain) to the correct days on a separate worksheet, based on the text and chart.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Interpret simple charts and graphs related to texts; Write responses to questions about informational texts. Grammar: Future simple tense for weather predictions, Prepositions of place. Vocabulary: <i>weather, forecast, report, cloud, storm, rain, wind, sun, temperature, prediction, day, morning, evening, afternoon, season, will be, predict, look like, bring, sunny, cloudy, warm, cold, rainy, windy, clear, dry, wet, hot, on, at, in</i>. Phonemic Awareness: Segmenting multisyllabic words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Cloze Reading):</b> Students receive a printed version of the weather report text with specific vocabulary words (e.g., <i>sunny, rainy, will be, temperature</i>) and prepositions (e.g., <i>in, on</i>) removed. They choose the correct words from a box to complete the sentences, referring to the weather chart for context. (e.g., "On Tuesday, the weather ____ cloudy. The <b>rain</b> ____ the east.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Segmenting Multisyllabic Words:</b> The teacher writes key multisyllabic vocabulary words from the text on the board (e.g., '<b>temperature</b>', '<b>prediction</b>', '<b>afternoon</b>', '<b>thermostat</b>'). Students clap and segment the words into syllables together. Then, in pairs, they read the words and practice segmenting them for each other, focusing on phonemic awareness.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Guided Reading Comprehension (Pair Work):</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a worksheet with 4-5 comprehension questions about the weather report text and chart (e.g., "What will the <b>temperature</b> be on Monday?", "Will it <b>rain</b> on Wednesday?", "What's the <b>forecast</b> for the <b>evening</b>?"). They read the questions and highlight the answers in the text and chart, then write down their responses using complete sentences. Teacher monitors and provides support.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Weather Report".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students will work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a new, slightly different, simple weather chart for a different city for 2-3 days (e.g., "Panama City Weather", "Boquete Weather").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative reading and simple report writing. Each group will read their assigned chart and collectively write a short, 3-sentence "Mini Weather Report" based on the data provided. They must use the future simple tense (e.g., "It <b>will be sunny</b> on Tuesday."), at least two weather vocabulary words (e.g., <i>sunshine, rain, wind</i>), and one preposition of place (e.g., "The <b>rain</b> is <b>in</b> the mountains."). They should also try to include one segmented multisyllabic word from their previous practice (e.g., '<b>temperature</b>' or '<b>forecast</b>').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Groups discuss the chart data, agree on their sentences, and write their report on a large piece of paper or whiteboard. The teacher encourages peer discussion and shared interpretation of the chart. Once finished, groups briefly present their written weather report to the class by one student reading it aloud. This reinforces reading fluency and peer discussion on weather-related topics (Sociolinguistic Competence).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group work and review of written mini weather reports. The teacher will circulate, noting student participation, ability to interpret the chart, and use of target language.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete an individual "Weather Chart Quick Check" worksheet. This worksheet presents a new, very simple, 2-day weather chart with icons and temperatures. Students must read the chart and answer two short questions in writing (e.g., "What will the weather be like on Day 1?", "Will it be <b>warm</b> or <b>cold</b> on Day 2?").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to interpret data from the chart (2 points).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of future simple tense in responses (1 point).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Legibility and clarity of written responses (1 point).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on a small slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can now read and understand a weather ______."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new word I learned today is ______."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of reading the weather report was easiest for me?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part was most difficult, and why?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> Today, the students successfully interpreted simple weather charts and graphs, and practiced segmenting multisyllabic words. The group activity fostered good collaboration, and most students grasped the future simple tense for predictions. Some students still need more practice with prepositions of place in context. I noticed some difficulty with longer multisyllabic words like 'thermostat' and 'prediction' for some students. For future lessons, I will use more visual cues for prepositions and revisit syllable segmentation with a fun game.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Speaking & Interaction), students will orally describe weather conditions and ask about the forecast based on their understanding from today's reading. We will use the weather reports they read today to create short dialogues.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>